          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"5-4\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_1\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"Most business actions are present, but the treasurer receipt-check is effectively duplicated/misgrouped, including an extra treasurer-check action after payment confirmation.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"Required precedence is broken: corrected reports do not return to supervisor review before further handling, and the path after payment confirmation continues to another treasurer check instead of ending.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The rejection and receipt-problem branches have wrong retry/return targets and unsupported exit routes; reports can bypass renewed supervisor approval, and the payment-confirmed branch does not terminate where required.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"mixed_or_cascading\",\"explanation\":\"The source requires any report returned for correction—whether rejected by the supervisor or sent back by the treasurer—to be corrected by the employee and then go again to a supervisor before proceeding. It also requires the process to end after payment confirmation. In the generated graph, both correction scenarios loop locally or exit to a merge that leads to treasurer checking/finalization, never back to supervisor review, and the payment-confirmation path continues to an additional treasurer-check step before ending. These are central whole-process routing errors, not mere notation differences, so the intended approval-and-resubmission behavior is not reliably preserved.\",\"overall_score\":0.24,\"severity\":\"major\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"ambiguity_flag\":false,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"\",\"requires_human_review\":false,\"review_reason\":\"\"}"
        }
